{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 3, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a member of the House of Toulouse and the Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187 he was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. \nThe *Early Life* of Raymond III is not well documented but it is known that he became the Count of Tripoli after the death of his father in 1152. \nHe played an important role in the *Politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem* and was a key figure in the court of King Baldwin III of Jerusalem. \nRaymond III was also involved in the *Military Campaigns* of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and fought against the Muslim forces of Nur ad-Din Zengi. \nIn 1164 Raymond III was captured by the Muslim forces while fighting in the Battle of Harim he was held prisoner for several years before being released in 1173. \nAfter his release Raymond III returned to Tripoli and continued to play an important role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. \nHe was a key figure in the *Succession Crisis* that followed the death of King Amalric I of Jerusalem in 1174. \nRaymond III supported the claim of Baldwin IV of Jerusalem to the throne and helped him to secure the crown. \nHe also played a key role in the *Battle of Montgisard* in 1177 where the Christian forces defeated the Muslim army of Saladin. \nRaymond III died in 1187 and was succeeded by his nephew Raymond IV Count of Tripoli. \nThe *Legacy of Raymond III* is still debated among historians but it is clear that he played an important role in the history of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the County of Tripoli. \nHe was a skilled military leader and a shrewd politician who was able to navigate the complex web of alliances and rivalries that characterized the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. \nThe *Impact of Raymond III on the Kingdom of Jerusalem* was significant and he helped to shape the course of the kingdom's history during a time o